  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Quite a bit of strato-cu out there this afternoon, \ndelineating the area where more abundant low-lvl moisture has \npooled.  Further west an area of ACCAS tied to modest mid-lvl theta-\ne advection continues to drift eastward.  We'll have to watch this \narea, but would not anticipate any more than sprinkles as it slides \neast later today. A light southeasterly wind is expected to continue \na push of upper 60 to lower 70 dewpoints northwest into portions of \nnorthwestern SD, where a focused risk for severe storms will develop \nlater this evening.  With the increased cloud cover and abundant \nwildfire smoke aloft, high temperatures may struggle to reach \nmuch past the middle 80s. \n\nTONIGHT:  Convection risks remain the highest concern into the \nevening and overnight hours.  Water vapor imagery this morning \nshowed a subtle shortwave trough moving through eastern MT, with \nthis waved modeled to dip southeast this evening. A deep instability \npool has pushed into western SD this afternoon, with upwards of 3000 \nJ/KG MLCAPE.  The issue may be some inhibition, impact of wildfire \nsmoke, and lack of a focused trigger which could delay widespread \ndevelopment. CAMS however continue to hint at deeper convection \nwaiting a few hours into the evening, and then triggering due to an \nincrease of 850:700 mb warm advection in the West River areas as \nstronger vorticity moves into the Dakotas. This northward LLJ should \nalso help push deeper instability further northeast over the eastern \nportions of SD. Where storms develop, signals will be strong for at \nleast 2\" hail, as elevated parcels will be within a corridor of 50 \nknots effective shear and should rotate. An increase of mid-lvl dry \nair will increase the potential for cold pool development in \nsupercells, eventually suggesting one or more larger clusters of \nstorms will form and try to track southeast after 10pm. Initial\nSCCRAM+HRRR guidance suggest rapidly increasing severe weather \nprobabilities as early as 0-2 UTC in central South Dakota. \nAfter this, HREF 2-5 km UH paintball plots all indicate a \ntendency for this activity to track southeast along the \ninstability axis reaching I- 29 around 12-1am and reaching the \nedge of the CWA around 4am. As stated earlier, initial updrafts \nmay have potential for 2\" hail, with greater risks for 50 to 70 \nmph winds in progressive clusters. This entire setup remains a \nbit uncertain, so it's imperative to continue monitoring the \nforecast. \n\nTUESDAY-TUESDAY NIGHT:  Behind any departing MCS we'll have a fair \ndegree of stability and subsidence that could leave stratus in place \nin the morning. Warming in the afternoon and another potential \nshortwave crossing the Rockies could promote strong to severe storms \nover western and central SD/ND by the evening with another MCS or \ntwo moving southeast overnight.  Guidance remains all over the place \nas it continues to struggle with resolving these embedded \nwaves. Nevertheless, SPC slight risks could end up extending \nfurther southeast in subsequent updates. \n\nWEDNESDAY-SUNDAY: Feel that most of Wednesday will again remain dry \nas shortwave ridging moves in and surface winds remain easterly. \nHigh temperatures will rise into the lower to mid 80s. The ridge \ncentered over the Four Corners and responsible for multiple days of \nNW flow will shift eastward by the second half of the work week. \nThis pattern shift will cause the ridge to flatten slightly in the \nNorthern Plains, inducing slight westerly to southwesterly flow \naloft into the weekend.  We'll be subject to smaller embedded lobes \nof vorticity tracking northeast from the southwestern CONUS and \nCentral Rockies.  Medium range guidance is not handling this \ntransition well, but seems to continue to bring nearly daily chances \nfor rain into the weekend.  At this point I highly doubt every day \nwill feature rain, but there will be some increased potential for \nmore showery activity at times Thursday and again late Friday into \nSaturday.  Instability looks low, so severe weather risks should \nalso remain low. Temperatures with more cloud cover and cooler \ninfluences of Canadian high pressure should trend into the middle \n70s into the weekend.",
